WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:04.250
It: Chapter Two has been screened for select
critics, and while there's still an embargo

00:00:04.250 --> 00:00:07.780
on formal reviews, many of them have taken
to Twitter to give their hot takes.

00:00:07.870 --> 00:00:13.040
So far, reactions are mixed, but most observers
agreed on two things: the film is wildly ambitious,

00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:15.380
and features an amazing performance from Bill
Hader.

00:00:15.570 --> 00:00:20.380
The movie and its predecessor, 2017's smash
hit It: Chapter One, are adapted from Stephen

00:00:20.390 --> 00:00:22.690
King's masterful 1986 novel.

00:00:22.690 --> 00:00:26.850
The first film followed a group of young outcasts
who dub themselves "The Losers' Club" facing

00:00:26.850 --> 00:00:30.770
off with an ancient, shape-shifting, evil
entity appearing most often in the guise of

00:00:30.770 --> 00:00:32.200
Pennywise, the Dancing Clown.

00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:36.420
It: Chapter Two focuses on the Losers' Club
as successful adults who are all called back

00:00:36.420 --> 00:00:40.840
to the small town of Derry, Maine, when "It"
emerges from Its slumber eager for a rematch.

00:00:40.949 --> 00:00:44.980
Initial critical reactions mostly praised
the flick's ambition in tackling one of King's

00:00:44.980 --> 00:00:47.860
most dense, challenging works, and largely
pulling it off.

00:00:47.860 --> 00:00:49.400
One Collider horror editor wrote,

00:00:49.550 --> 00:00:53.620
"[IT Chapter Two] is big and ambitious and
can't quite handle the weight of its aspirations,

00:00:53.629 --> 00:00:55.710
but it's still a fine piece of heartfelt horror.

00:00:55.710 --> 00:00:59.739
The parts that work, work REALLY well and
Bill Hader is every-bit the scene-stealing

00:00:59.740 --> 00:01:01.340
standout we all hoped he'd be."

00:01:01.370 --> 00:01:05.160
Hader — who plays the motor-mouthed Richie,
portrayed as a child by Stranger Things' Finn

00:01:05.160 --> 00:01:08.580
Wolfhard — was the cast member most consistently
singled out for praise.

00:01:08.810 --> 00:01:13.360
The Saturday Night Live alum and star of the
HBO's dark comedy series Barry has never taken

00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:17.520
on a role in a horror project before, and
by all accounts, he absolutely kills it in

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:18.020
this one.

00:01:18.160 --> 00:01:22.880
Fandango managing editor Erik Davis was also
among the chorus of voices singing Hader's

00:01:22.880 --> 00:01:24.120
praises, writing in part,

00:01:24.120 --> 00:01:28.450
"[IT Chapter Two] plays heavier in tone, dealing
[with] defeating the burden of childhood trauma,

00:01:28.450 --> 00:01:30.500
[and] I definitely teared up at the very end.

00:01:30.500 --> 00:01:31.780
Bill Hader steals it."

00:01:31.960 --> 00:01:35.120
Even those critics who weren't particularly
impressed with the film were quick to lavish

00:01:35.130 --> 00:01:39.600
praise on Hader, with CinemaBlend's Sean O'Connell
offering up pretty much the highest compliment

00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:40.630
that could be paid.

00:01:40.630 --> 00:01:41.120
He wrote,

00:01:41.320 --> 00:01:43.860
"So, [IT Chapter Two]: it's [very] good, but
not great.

00:01:43.860 --> 00:01:45.320
I liked it, but didn't LOVE it.

00:01:45.560 --> 00:01:47.080
First movie is a tough act to follow.

00:01:47.080 --> 00:01:49.880
TWO is gruesome, strange but rarely scary.

00:01:50.130 --> 00:01:53.460
Nails important aspects from the book, but
feels overlong.

00:01:53.460 --> 00:01:56.560
Cast is aces, though, [and] Bill Hader SHOULD
be in Oscar convo."

00:01:56.560 --> 00:01:59.180
Of course, there were some critics who simply
panned the film.

00:01:59.420 --> 00:02:00.560
Critic Scott Menzel wrote,

00:02:00.780 --> 00:02:03.300
"It: Chapter Two is the biggest disappointment
of the year.

00:02:03.440 --> 00:02:06.920
A dull [and] bloated mess that spends too
much time rehashing things from the first

00:02:06.920 --> 00:02:07.420
film.

00:02:07.580 --> 00:02:11.320
While there are a few good moments, the film
ultimately lacks suspense and scares.

00:02:11.320 --> 00:02:13.220
The performances are also a mixed bag."

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:17.610
Uproxx senior entertainment writer Mike Ryan
had a different gripe, and although it's apparent

00:02:17.610 --> 00:02:20.489
he's not the biggest fan of the novel, he
should probably take that up with Stephen

00:02:20.489 --> 00:02:20.980
King.

00:02:21.040 --> 00:02:21.540
He wrote,

00:02:21.540 --> 00:02:26.099
"I am not an IT expert but I get the sense,
even in the book, IT's powers/motivations

00:02:26.100 --> 00:02:27.099
aren't always well defined.

00:02:27.220 --> 00:02:30.800
That vagueness works with the kids, but I
wish this one had some basic rules?

00:02:31.080 --> 00:02:35.360
Because it feels like IT could kill any of
these people at anytime and just doesn't."

00:02:35.379 --> 00:02:39.170
For the most part, though, critics acknowledged
that the one-two punch of the It movies constitutes

00:02:39.170 --> 00:02:43.120
a feat that very few filmmakers have been
able to pull off over the last few decades

00:02:43.120 --> 00:02:47.769
or so a very successful King adaptation, one
that sports a deep knowledge of and affinity

00:02:47.769 --> 00:02:49.120
for the Master's writing style.

00:02:49.340 --> 00:02:52.840
Fans will have to wait for the actual reviews
to come rolling in before they can get a more

00:02:52.840 --> 00:02:55.639
detailed analysis of the flick's strengths
and weaknesses.

00:02:55.639 --> 00:03:00.419
But, two things seem certain: It: Chapter
Two swings for the fences, and Hader is the

00:03:00.420 --> 00:03:01.100
film's MVP.

00:03:01.420 --> 00:03:06.109
In addition to Hader, the movie's impressive
cast includes James McAvoy , Isaiah Mustafa,

00:03:06.109 --> 00:03:09.620
Jessica Chastain, James Ransone, and, of course,
Bill Skarsgård.

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:11.480
“haha.”

